=09
<h1 align=3D"center">The proof of climate change <br />
		and the inevitability of 2oC.</h1>
=09
<p align=3D"center">Prepared by Dr John James for<br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">The Climate Crisis Coalition</span><b=
r />
		<a href=3D"http://www.planetextinction.com/">www.planetextinction.com</=
a></p>
=09
<p>The&nbsp;benign climate that has allowed the human race to multiply, d=
evelop and prosper has remained more or less unchanged for 10,000 years. =
Its stability accounts for the entire span of civilised human history.<a =
id=3D"_ednref1" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn1" name=3D"_ednref1">[1]</a></p>
=09
<p>Since the 1780s the average global temperature has risen 0.78oC from a=
 one-third increase in atmospheric CO2.<a id=3D"_ednref2" title=3D"" href=
=3D"#_edn2" name=3D"_ednref2"> [2 ]</a> To this should be added methane a=
nd other gasses,<a id=3D"_ednref3" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn3" name=3D"_ed=
nref3"> [3 ]</a> and the additional impact of water vapour.<a id=3D"_ednr=
ef4" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn4" name=3D"_ednref4"> [ 4]</a> </p>
=09
<h2>For the following reasons a 2oC rise is now inevitable: </h2>
=09
<p>Were we to stop all emissions immediately, temperature would continue =
to rise without any more input from us from latent heat<a id=3D"_ednref5"=
 title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn5" name=3D"_ednref5"> [ 5]</a> and aerosol dimmi=
ng.<a id=3D"_ednref6" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn6" name=3D"_ednref6"> [6 ]<=
/a> </p>
=09
<p>Latent heat commits the planet to about 0.45oC more;<a id=3D"_ednref7"=
 title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn7" name=3D"_ednref7"> [7 ]</a> Aerosols shield u=
s from a further 20%. <br />
		Together these are holding back the full impact of the CO2-e in the atm=
osphere by twenty or thirty years. The increase so far reflects only what=
 has been emitted up to the later 1970s. </p>
=09
<p>The calculation is simple: Were we to instantly stop all emissions, st=
op everything today, average global temperature would continue to rise as=
 follows: Current temp + latent heat + dimming =3D 0.78 + 0.45 + 20% =3D =
1.5oC. <br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">This is double the increase of the pa=
st two centuries</span>.</p>
=09
<p>It is therefore <span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">inevitable</span>, =
from what we have already emitted, that food production will decline, dro=
ughts will spread and species will become extinct. There will be more hun=
ger, less available fresh water, the seas will rise and vast numbers of r=
efugees will be on the move.<a id=3D"_ednref8" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn8"=
 name=3D"_ednref8"> [ 8]</a> These conclusions are supported in the May 2=
007 Bangkok IPCC report.<a id=3D"_ednref9" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn9" nam=
e=3D"_ednref9"> [9 ]</a></p>
=09
<p>In addition, it leaves a margin of only 0.5oC before we reach the crit=
ical threshold.<a id=3D"_ednref10" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn10" name=3D"_e=
dnref10"> [10 ]</a> Above 2oC it is believed that global heat would start=
 to trigger the release of the greenhouse gases now stored in the oceans,=
 in trees and in the soil.<a id=3D"_ednref11" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn11"=
 name=3D"_ednref11"> [11]</a> Once started the process is unstoppable and=
 could further increase average global temperature by 6oC or more.<a id=3D=
"_ednref12" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn12" name=3D"_ednref12"> [12 ]</a> </p=
>
=09
<h2>How close are we to a 2oC rise, and when will we get there? </h2>
=09
<p>2oC is closer than most are prepared to admit. <a id=3D"topic2" name=3D=
"topic2">[13]</a>Considering that no one knows for sure what temperature =
would trigger the tipping points, we are already in a zone of absolute ri=
sk. </p>
=09
<p>Over the past three years the average annual increase in CO2 has been =
nearly 3 ppm, almost three times what it was in the 1990s.<a id=3D"_ednre=
f13" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn13" name=3D"_ednref13"> [14 ]</a> The annual=
 rate is still rising from new coal-fired plants in all countries, especi=
ally China,<a id=3D"_ednref14" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn14" name=3D"_ednre=
f14"> [15 ]</a> from more vehicles and logging and mega-bushfires.</p>
=09
<p>In the next ten years global emissions will add enough to make a 2oC r=
ise inevitable, and with it catastrophic climate change over the followin=
g decades. The passing of this threshold will be of the most enormous sig=
nificance. We will have entered the era of dangerous climate change where=
 neither we nor our children can count on a safe future.</p>
=09
<h2>The tipping points:</h2>
=09
<p>The research of the past five or so years shows that nearly all earth'=
s systems known to effect climate change have begun the process of positi=
ve feedback. There are certain points "of no return" after which unstoppa=
ble heating will become irreversible. A report in May 2007 shows this has=
 already started.<a id=3D"_ednref15" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn15" name=3D"=
_ednref15"> [15 ]</a> The evidence is that these events occur very rapidl=
y, often in less than a decade.<a id=3D"_ednref16" title=3D"" href=3D"#_e=
dn16" name=3D"_ednref16"> [16 ]</a> The major tipping points are:</p>
=09
<ol>
	=09
<li>Glaciers collapse<a id=3D"_ednref17" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn17" name=
=3D"_ednref17"> [17]</a> and raise sea levels enough to inundate world se=
a ports<a id=3D"_ednref18" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn18" name=3D"_ednref18"=
> [18 ]</a> and reflect less heat into space.<a id=3D"_ednref19" title=3D=
"" href=3D"#_edn19" name=3D"_ednref19"> [19 ]</a> Earthquakes will follow=
 as the weight comes off the land.<a id=3D"_ednref20" title=3D"" href=3D"=
#_edn20" name=3D"_ednref20"> [20 ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Methane clathrates held under the sea begin to burp.<a id=3D"_ednref2=
1" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn21" name=3D"_ednref21"> 21[ ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Permafrost thaws and releases vast quantities of methane.<a id=3D"_ed=
nref22" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn22" name=3D"_ednref22"> [22 ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Forests and soils no longer absorb carbon, but become emission source=
s.<a id=3D"_ednref23" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn23" name=3D"_ednref23"> [23=
 ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Logging and bush fires increase the carbon load.<a id=3D"_ednref24" t=
itle=3D"" href=3D"#_edn24" name=3D"_ednref24"> [24 ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Drought degrades the Amazon forest, so releasing its carbon.<a id=3D"=
_ednref25" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn25" name=3D"_ednref25"> [25 ]</a> </li=
>
	=09
<li>Disastrous weather and heat could bankrupt the insurance industry,<a =
id=3D"_ednref26" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn26" name=3D"_ednref26"> [26 ]</a=
> and with it one foundation of personal and commercial security. </li>
	=09
<li>Decline of cold fresh water from the Arctic<a id=3D"_ednref27" title=3D=
"" href=3D"#_edn27" name=3D"_ednref27"> [27 ]</a> alters the Gulf Stream.=
<a id=3D"_ednref28" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn28" name=3D"_ednref28"> [28 ]=
</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Hotter oceans absorb less carbon,<a id=3D"_ednref29" title=3D"" href=3D=
"#_edn29" name=3D"_ednref29"> [29 ]</a> and acidification is killing the =
fisheries.<a id=3D"_ednref30" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn30" name=3D"_ednref=
30"> [30 ]</a> </li>
	=09
<li>Drought, heat, pests and disease disrupt global food supplies.<a id=3D=
"_ednref31" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn31" name=3D"_ednref31"> [31 ]</a> </l=
i>
	=09
<li>The human tipping point: culture shock,<a id=3D"_ednref32" title=3D""=
 href=3D"#_edn32" name=3D"_ednref32"> [32 ]</a> refugees,<a id=3D"_ednref=
33" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn33" name=3D"_ednref33"> [33 ]</a> wars<a id=3D=
"_ednref34" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn34" name=3D"_ednref34"> [34 ]</a> for=
 water, land, food and shelter and the probable death of billions.<a id=3D=
"_ednref35" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn35" name=3D"_ednref35"> [35 ]</a> Nuk=
es could worsen climate.<a id=3D"_ednref36" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn36" n=
ame=3D"_ednref36"> [36 ]</a> </li>
	</ol>
=09
<p>There would be minimal economic downturn if we prepare how to change o=
ur ways now.<a id=3D"_ednref37" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn37" name=3D"_ednr=
ef37"> [37 ]</a></p>
=09
<h2><span style=3D"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">Present policies</span><span style=3D=
"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">:</span> </h2>
=09
<p>The current consensus is politically driven, and is irrelevant. Most c=
oncerned politicians and green groups are satisfied with demanding we cut=
 existing emissions by 60 percent or so in the next <span style=3D"FONT-W=
EIGHT: bold">40 years. The above calculation shows we need to achieve muc=
h more than that in the next ten.</span></p>
=09
<p>Emission trading and carbon taxes are based on the assumption that the=
 rich can go on living as we do with minimal adjustments to the system. T=
he rush to =E7=A5=96lean coal and sequestration follows the same rational=
e.<a id=3D"_ednref38" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn38" name=3D"_ednref38"> [38=
 ]</a> Long-term these are useful only if we have a survival strategy alr=
eady in place.</p>
=09
<p>The market-based approach diverts investment from renewable technology=
. It maintains our standard of living while continuing to use fossil fuel=
s to maintain our consumerist society. </p>
=09
<h2>Conclusion from the above argument:</h2>
=09
<p>To keep below that threshold the world needs to c<span style=3D"FONT-W=
EIGHT: bold">ut emissions by 10 percent every year over the next 10 years=
, starting now. </span>The major items needing attention are (in order) c=
oal, vehicles, cement, aeroplanes, and shopping malls. Most people don=E7=
=A8=9A yet recognise the importance of the last three. And this makes no =
mention of emissions from the military.</p>
=09
<p>In the final analysis this comes down to transforming our consumer soc=
iety. If we would all buy less and travel less, we would emit less.<a id=3D=
"_ednref39" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn39" name=3D"_ednref39"> [39 ]</a> Thi=
s is why the public has to be involved.</p>
=09
<p><span style=3D"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">Since these changes are revolutionar=
y, and are pointless without some global consensus, we need to assume tha=
t some nations will not change in time, and that we should prepare on our=
 own. </span>Part of this would be to cut emissions by a significant amou=
nt every year for the next decade, by reducing personal consumption and b=
y strongly encouraging renewable technologies. </p>
=09
<h2>Preparing for our national security:</h2>
=09
<p>Since neither we nor the world will change from coal in time, sequestr=
ation and nuclear and renewables are less important than planning how thi=
s country and her people can cope as the climate becomes seriously worse.=
 For that we need to prepare our governments, public opinion and communit=
y infrastructures to deal with it.</p>
=09
<p>We believe Australia is one of the few countries that could survive th=
e coming disintegration as we can seal our borders, have plentiful resour=
ces and an educated citizenry. </p>
=09
<p>The following are a few of the consequences to Australia of rapid clim=
ate change. Risk assessment<a id=3D"_ednref40" title=3D"" href=3D"#_edn40=
" name=3D"_ednref40"> [40 ]</a> &nbsp;would need to include the following=
:</p>
=09
<p>&nbsp;&nbsp;<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">Sea level rise: </span>=
docks flooded, reduced trade, oil, import or export of food<br />
		&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Wate=
rfront houses, roads, sewage, communication, fresh water, infrastructure<=
br />
		&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Inte=
rnal distribution of food, essential services, power.<br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;Violent weather:</span> =
cyclones down the east coast, prolonged heat waves, hail and drought. <br=
 />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;Fire and flood:</span> h=
ospitals, repairs, insurance, mortgages, disaster management.<br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;Diseases migrate </span>=
<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">south:</span> Ross River, malaria, cho=
lera, West Nile, available hospitals.<br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;Internal refugees:</span=
> housing and resettlement, petrol, law and order, community structures.<=
br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;External refugees:</span=
> military, diplomatic, Fortress Australia, peace keeping, war, nukes.<br=
 />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">Culture shock:</span> public transpo=
rt, travel to shops and work, change jobs, mortgages<br />
		&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;lower standard of living: new technologies, cha=
nged life styles, reduced advertising <br />
		&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;An unaware public will be less able to face it,=
 and may polarise.<br />
		&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Local manufacture: electric cars, busses, train=
s, power tools, white goods <br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-WEIGHT: bold">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Develop a nat=
ional resilience to a total shift in life expectations</span><br />
		<span style=3D"FONT-STYLE: italic">&nbsp;&nbsp;Ethics:</span> immigrati=
on, rationing, mandatory ad draconian political control.</p>
=09
<p>We recommend the immediate establishment of a national Risk Assessment=
 Council that would appoint Expert Committees to assess each of these iss=
ues and prepare preliminary recommendations. Since time is of the essence=
 all committee investigations should be completed in the first six months=
. </p>
=09
<p>As one purpose should be to prepare citizens for the changes that are =
coming and to support their confidence in the willingness of government t=
o protect and nurture them, all steps in these investigations should be m=
ade public.</p>
=09
<p>Australians should be educated in the risks of climate change so they =
may be as prepared as possible in their private and commercial lives, and=
 do so with the confidence that government is facing the issue and determ=
ined to deal with it for the common good.</p>
=09
<div class=3D"style2">
	=09
<div id=3D"edn1">
		=09
<p><a id=3D"_edn1" title=3D"" href=3D"#_ednref1" name=3D"_edn1">[1 ]</a> =
